{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Elohim spoke.</b> There are difficult questions in this pericope. Many said that HaShem spoke only the first two commandments, and their proof is that in the first utterance it is written, אנכי ה׳ אלהיך (v. 2), and in the second, כי אנכי ה׳ אלהיך אל קנא (v. 5), and in the third it is written את שם ה׳ אלהיך (v. 7), also את אשר ישא את שמו לשוא (ibid.), and he did not say שמי, and in the fourth, כי ששת ימים עשה ה׳ etc., על כן ברך ה׳ (v. 11), and in the fifth he said אשר ה׳ אלהיך נותן לך (v. 12). And one may ask how the utterance אנכי is counted among the Ten Commandments, for it is the command of the commander, and it is neither a positive commandment nor a negative commandment — and questions harder than these. Now, we have read this pericope, which is the pericope of וישמע יתרו, as primary, and the pericope of ואתחנן as secondary. We found that from the beginning of אנכי until the end of את אשר ישא את שמו לשוא (v. 7), there is no difference between the two pericopes. But from the beginning of זכור until the end of the Ten Commandments, there is variation in every place: in the first, זכור (v. 8), and in the second, שמור (Deuteronomy 5:12). Also there, at the end of the latter, there is an addition: כאשר צוך ה׳ אלהיך (ibid. v. 16), not present in the first. In the first, ובהמתך (v. 10), and in the second, an addition: ושורך וחמורך (Deut. 5:14). And harder than all these: in the first, the reason for Sabbath is written — כי ששת ימים עשה ה׳ את השמים ואת הארץ, and it further says, על כן ברך ה׳ את יום השבת (v. 11) — and these verses are not written in the second; rather, a different reason: וזכרת כי עבד היית בארץ מצרים, and at the end it says, על כן צוך ה׳ אלהיך לעשות את יום השבת (Deut. 5:15). In the first, the reward for honoring father and mother is written: למען יאריכון ימיך (v. 12), and likewise in the second, except he added ולמען ייטב לך (Deut. 5:16). Also he added regarding honoring father and mother: כאשר צוך ה׳ אלהיך (ibid.). In the first it is written לא תרצח לא תנאף לא תגנוב לא תענה (v. 13); in the second it is written לא תרצח ולא תנאף ולא תגנוב ולא תענה (Deut. 5:17). In the first it is written עד שקר (v. 13); in the second, עד שוא (Deut. 5:17). In the first it is written לא תחמוד בית רעך לא תחמד אשת רעך (v. 14); in the second, ולא תחמד אשת רעך ולא תתאוה בית רעך (Deut. 5:18). In the first, שדהו is not written; in the second, שדהו is written (ibid.). In the first, עבדו ואמתו precede שורו וחמורו; in the second, שורו וחמורו come before עבדו ואמתו. In the first it is written: וידבר אלהים את כל הדברים האלה לאמר; in the second: את הדברים האלה דבר ה׳ אל כל קהלכם (Deut. 5:19). When we searched the words of the Sages, may their memory be blessed, regarding all this, we found that they said: זכור ושמור בדבור אחד נאמרו — they were spoken in a single utterance. And this utterance is more difficult than all the difficulties we have, as I shall explain. Heaven forbid that I should say they did not speak correctly, for our intellect is slight compared to their intellect. Rather, the people of our generation will think that their words are as they sound, but it is not so, as I shall explain at the end after I mention the difficulties. And ultimately I shall explain the straight path to remove all the difficulties and questions in this pericope. For it is not possible that זכור and שמור were said simultaneously except by way of miracle. Now, we must concede that even here one may ask: why was זכור ושמור not written in the first? And likewise in the second. And what do we do with those verses — if they were said simultaneously like זכור ושמור? And why did the Sages, of blessed memory, not mention this? For there is far greater reason to wonder how many verses with different meanings were said simultaneously, more than two words whose meaning is one and equal, that they should be said simultaneously. And how could HaShem say כאשר צוך ה׳ אלהיך (Deut. 5:16) — and when did he command before this assembly regarding honoring father and mother? And in the first, למען ייטב לך is not written. If so, he said this but not that. And how could he say לא תנאף with a vav, and also לא תחמד בית רעך (v. 14), and in the second ולא תחמד אשת רעך (Deut. 5:16)? And furthermore, how could he say עבדו ואמתו and afterward שורו וחמורו, and say the reverse simultaneously? The intellect cannot bear all these things. And harder than all that I have mentioned: every wonder that was performed through Moses has, for part of it, an analogy — and the discerning will understand. Now this matter is the wonder of wonders, that HaShem placed זכור and שמור simultaneously. And it would have been fitting for this to be written and explained in the Torah more than all the signs and wonders that were written. And if we say that the speech of HaShem is not like the speech of any man — then how did Israel understand the speech of HaShem? For a man, if he hears זכור and שמור simultaneously, will understand neither this nor that. And even a single word, such as זכור: if he does not hear the zayin before the kof and the resh, he will not understand what the speaker is saying. Now we know that the perception of the eye is weightier than the perception of the ear, for we know by complete proofs that the instant the eye sees the lightning is the instant of the thunder — only the eye sees from afar, and the air brings the sound to the ear, and its travel is slow, and it does not reach the ear except after the passing of an instant. And the letters that a person articulates — their form is inscribed in the air according to their point of emission from the five places, and not in the manner of script written by human hand. Now every letter zayin enters the ear before kof, vav, resh. If we say it was a wonder that זכור ושמור were said simultaneously — how would the ear hear? And if we say it was also a wonder that the ear heard two words simultaneously, unlike its habit of hearing two letters — why did the Sages, of blessed memory, not mention this, which is weightier than simultaneous speech? And what do we do with the remaining difficulties arising from the variation of the verses whose meaning is not one, like זכור ושמור, and what is written in the second but not in the first? Also, how do we reconcile לא תחמוד בית רעך with ולא תחמד אשת רעך, also the reversed order? I cannot explain all these to you until I explain to you the discipline of the language of the Holy Tongue. And HaShem is my witness, and knows my secret. For were it not that I needed to explain these difficulties, I would keep silent. And before I explain, I shall set right the words of the Sages, of blessed memory:" "<b>Says Abraham the author.</b> The practice of the speakers of the Holy Tongue: sometimes they explain their discourse very well, and sometimes they state the intent in brief words so that the listener can understand their meaning. And know that words are like bodies, and meanings are like souls, and the body is to the soul like a vessel. Therefore the practice of all the wise in every language is to preserve the meanings, and they are not concerned with variation of the words, as long as they are equivalent in meaning. Now I shall give you analogies. HaShem said to Cain: ארור אתה מן האדמה … כי תעבוד את האדמה לא תוסף תת כחה לך, נע ונד תהיה בארץ (Gen. 4:11–12), and Cain said: הן גרשת אותי היום מעל פני האדמה (ibid. v. 14). And one who has no understanding might think that the meaning is not the same because of the variation in words. Eliezer said: הגמאיני נא (Gen. 24:17), and he also said: ואומר אליה השקיני נא (ibid. v. 45). Moses said: בכור השבי אשר בבית הבור (above 12:29), and it is written: בכור השפחה אשר אחר הריחים (ibid. 11:5). Moses mentioned the prayer in Deuteronomy that he prayed on Israel's behalf because of the calf, and it is not identical to the prayer mentioned in the pericope of כי תשא — to one who has no understanding. And the general principle: every dream, like Pharaoh's and Nebuchadnezzar's, and many after them — you will find differing words but equivalent meaning. And as I told you that sometimes they take the short path and sometimes the long, so too they sometimes add a servile letter or remove it, and the matter is the same. HaShem said: ותכלת וארגמן (below 25:4), and Moses said: תכלת וארגמן (below 28:6). HaShem said: אבני שהם (below 25:7), and Moses said: ואבני שהם (below 35:9). And many like these. And both are correct, for what is written without a vav took the short path, and it does no harm; and what is written with a vav also does no harm, because he added to explain. The vav, which is visible in the pronunciation of the mouth — no one seeks a reason why it is removed, why it is written, why it is added, for both this and that are correct. Now regarding what is visible and pronounced, one does not seek a reason for it in writing. If so, why do we seek a reason for a quiescent letter that is not pronounced — such as the word לעולם: why is it written plene, or why defective? The people of this generation seek a reason for plene as well as defective. If they sought a reason for only one of them, or if the practice were to write all one way, I would keep silent. Now I shall give you a parable. Someone said to me: Write to my friend, and this is what is written: אני פלוני אוהבך לעולם — and I wrote פלני without a vav, אהבך also without a vav, לעלם defective. Reuben came and asked me: why did you write them defective? I have no need to write except what he told me, and I have no preference whether they are plene or defective. Perhaps Levi will come and inform me how I should write, and I would not want to prolong — but the discerning will understand. And now I shall explain to you the aforementioned questions:" "Know that the Ten Commandments as they are written in this pericope — HaShem spoke them all, for it is written: וידבר אלהים את כל הדברים האלה לאמר, and their beginning is אנכי and their end is וכל אשר לרעך. And Moses too said, when he mentioned the Ten Commandments a second time: את הדברים האלה דבר ה׳ אל כל קהלכם (Deut. 5:19), and there is no interruption between them. And furthermore, when they were all written by the finger of Elohim, so too HaShem said them all. And the proof texts they brought — that what is written in the third, fourth, and fifth utterances is not in the manner of the speaker — one must answer: After you have accepted זה השם להיות לך לאלהים, therefore he said: לא תשא את שם ה׳ אלהיך לשוא (v. 7). Also: כי ששת ימים עשה ה׳ (v. 11). And furthermore, the practice of speakers of the Holy Tongue is to speak thus: it is written לא ילין חלב חגי (below 23:18), and after it is written: תביא בית ה׳ אלהיך (ibid. v. 19), and many like these. And those who say that the utterance אנכי is not among the Ten Commandments — on this they disagreed: some say לא יהיה לך is the first utterance and the second is לא תעשה לך פסל. This is not correct, for it is one matter, whether in secret or in public, whether in faith of the heart or in deed. In the utterance of זכור, you, your son, your daughter — in secret and in public — and it is all one commandment. And others said that לא תחמוד בית רעך is the ninth utterance and the tenth is לא תחמוד אשת רעך, and their proof is that HaShem said לא תחמוד twice, for desire pertains to two things: one issues to action, like theft and its equivalent, such as ולא יחמד איש את ארצך (below 34:24) — for if that desire were in the heart, it would be no evil. And לא תחמוד the second is in the heart; therefore Moses needed to explain לא תתאוה. This too is words of vanity, for what reason would he mention theft of a house alone, and not the others with it? I shall give you a proof from the words of Moses that they did not speak correctly. HaShem said לא תחמוד בית רעך, and Moses said לא תחמד אשת רעך (Deut. 5:18). According to their view, the second לא תחמוד written in this pericope is in the heart, and the first is theft of the house. Now Moses reversed the matter, for he said with ולא תתאוה: בית רעך, and he said with לא תחמוד, which is theft: אשת רעך. Their reasoning is thus corrupted. And the truth is that the utterance אנכי is the first, as I shall explain in its place." "And now I shall speak of זכור ושמור. Know that the meanings are preserved, not the words. Isaac our father said to Esau: בעבור תברכך נפשי בטרם אמות (Gen. 27:4), and Rebekah, who heard what he said, told Jacob: ואברכך לפני ה׳ לפני מותי (ibid. v. 7) — like בטרם אמות. If so, what is the reason for adding לפני ה׳? The answer: she knew that Isaac was a prophet of HaShem, and the blessing he would give was by way of prophecy. Therefore she said to Jacob לפני ה׳, and it is as if she is explaining the reason for the blessing. So too did Moses. For the Ten Commandments written in this pericope are the words of HaShem without addition or diminution, and they alone are what is written on the tablets of the covenant — not as the Gaon [Saadia] said, that זכור is on one tablet and שמור on the second. And the Ten Commandments written in the pericope of ואתחנן are the words of Moses, and the absolute proof is that there it is written twice: כאשר צוך ה׳ אלהיך. And know that in the posterior of the brain of the head is the faculty of memory, and that place is the place of safekeeping of forms. Now memory encompasses observance, and the meaning of זכור is that one should remember every day which day of the week it is — and all this in order that he observe the seventh day and do no work in it. The meaning of memory is observance. And when HaShem said זכור, all who heard understood that its meaning is like שמור, as if they were said simultaneously. And Moses did not need to mention a second time the reason כי ששת ימים עשה ה׳, for at the beginning he said כאשר צוך ה׳ אלהיך, as if to say: as written in your Torah in the utterance of זכור (v. 8) through ויקדשהו (v. 11). And because HaShem commanded that the slave and the maidservant rest, and did not explain why, Moses explained its reason and said: This that HaShem commanded you — that the slave rest — is because you shall remember that you were a slave like him in Egypt, and HaShem redeemed you. And know that the eight commandments are all negative commandments, and the utterance of honoring parents is a positive commandment. By the balance of intellect that HaShem planted in the heart of man, every person is obligated to do good to one who does good to him. The son would not have come into the world except through his parents; they nourished him, raised him, educated him, gave him drink and food, and clothed him. He is obligated to honor them all his days, for they were the cause of his being alive on the face of the earth. Therefore his reward is למען יאריכון ימיך. And because HaShem breathed into him a soul through the agency of the parents who prepared the body, he commanded that he honor his parents, for one who honors them honors HaShem. Moses explained and said: כבד את אביך ואת אמך, and he said כאשר צוך ה׳ אלהיך (Deut. 5:16) — for the reward is upon a positive commandment, and the punishment upon a negative commandment, as I shall explain in the pericope of לא תאכלנו (ibid. 12:25). Now Moses said: You are obligated to honor parents למען יאריכון ימיך, and this obligation is by the balance of intellect. Also, because HaShem commanded that you honor them, there will be for you an additional good reward, namely למען ייטב לך, because you hearkened to the voice of HaShem. Now the words of Moses are as explained in the words of Rebekah. And I have already said that אבני שהם and ואבני שהם are equivalent; likewise לא תנאף and ולא תנאף; and שוא and שקר are children of one father; also תחמוד and תתאוה issued from one womb. And HaShem said לא תחמוד בית רעך, for men of understanding acquire a house first, then a wife, then slave and maidservant, then ox and donkey to plow his field — and so they are ordered in this pericope. And Moses ordered them differently, for young men desire a wife first, then a house; and corresponding to the house and the field, he put ox and donkey first, with which to plow, and afterward slave and maidservant. And he concluded at the end: וכל אשר לרעך, just as HaShem concluded. And because in the words of Moses the words of HaShem are intermingled, therefore it is written את הדברים (Deut. 5:19) and not כל הדברים as is written in the first."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {3 "Ibn Ezra quotes Saadia's view that זכור and שמור were written on separate tablets, but the argumentative context makes clear he rejects this."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T20:26:45.114217963Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}